{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** The requirement states one obligation: each link from a bundled skill to a file it links to MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill upholds this on its own, so it is a site obligation. The MAY clause imposes nothing to check. No obligation is left that the sites don't cover, and no particular code carries one out.\n\n**Site query `skills`.** `run_named_query requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills` returned five sites. They are the `SKILL.md` files of `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor` under `assets/skills`.\n\nI searched for `\\A---\\nname:`, which matches any file that starts with skill front matter. It found exactly those five files, so the query misses no skill. The reference files under `references/` don't start with `name:` front matter, so the query selects nothing outside the requirement's scope.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#const[SKILLS]` lists the same five skills.\n\n**Query snippets.** The match snippet is front matter followed by a heading, which is what a skill file looks like. The no_match snippet is a plain heading with no front matter, which is the nearest thing the query must not select. That is adequate for a one-condition rule.\n\n**Violation queries.** The text declares none.\n\n**Supporting test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]` carries a `{test}` reference to the requirement. It does the following:\n- It pins the when-to-read sentence for every link from a `SKILL.md`.\n- It pins the guidance for pointers inside reference files.\n- It checks that every `references/*.md` pointer in every skill falls inside one of those checked sentences.\n- It checks that every reference file of every skill has at least one checked link.\n\nThe obligation is reviewed per site, so this test is supporting evidence and I did not assess it further.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions.references",
        "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
